  "id": "DrugBank_DB06824 - MONDO_7763",
  "verbalization": "Triethylenetetramine is implicated in the pathogenesis of Nonpapillary Renal Cell Carcinoma through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Triethylenetetramine may act as a potential therapeutic agent for this disease, as it is associated with Clear Cell Renal Carcinoma and Renal Cell Adenocarcinoma, both of which are closely related to Nonpapillary Renal Cell Carcinoma. The drug’s involvement in the treatment of Clear Cell Renal Carcinoma is supported by the indication chain to Cabozantinib and Sirolimus, which are known to target pathways involved in tumor growth and progression. Tivozanib, another agent linked to Nonpapillary Renal Cell Carcinoma, is also part of the indication chain, suggesting a possible role for Triethylenetetramine in modulating similar pathways. The integrated evidence implies that Triethylenetetramine may exert its effects through mechanisms that inhibit tumor growth or suppress cellular proliferation, potentially offering a novel therapeutic approach for this malignancy. The strength of the evidence lies in the consistent indications across different pathways, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
